## Entries

### 2026-08-25 - Add the article extraction module and its fixture tests (commit 3c69185)

**What changed:** Added `spike/extract.js` with `SELECTORS` and
`extractArticleMeta(doc)`. Replaced the smoke test in `spike/extract.test.mjs`
with a loop that runs one test per article entry in `manifest.json`, plus a test
that an empty document returns nulls and does not throw. Corrected the title row
in `spike/README.md` from `og.title` to `og:title`.

**Why:** The README table is the human record of the read paths. `sources` is the
machine record: `extractArticleMeta` returns the label of the path that supplied
each field, so a later fixture test can assert which fallback fired, not only the
value. The two records must agree, and the title row disagreed by one character.
The colon is the real distinction: `og:title` is the `property` attribute value
that `meta[property="og:title"]` selects on, and `og.title` selects nothing.

**Files:** `spike/extract.js` (new), `spike/extract.test.mjs`, `spike/README.md`,
`docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-16-milestone-0.md`, `changes.log`.

**Verification:** `cd spike; npm test` passes 3 of 3. `extractArticleMeta` run
over both fixtures returns the same `sources` object for each:
`{wordCount: ".body.markup", title: "og:title", author: "meta:author",
publication: "ld:publisher.name", canonicalUrl: "link:canonical"}`. Word counts
are 1599 free and 684 paywalled. `isPreview` is `false` free and `true`
paywalled.

**State after:** Article extraction works against both fixtures. The Saved list
half of the spike does not exist: no `saved-list.html`, no
`extractSavedEntries`. `spike/README.md` still has four empty cells in the Saved
list table.

**Next up:** Task 5, Step 1: find the Saved list URL.

**Open questions:** `extractArticleMeta` derives `isPreview` from the `.paywall`
selector, but `spike/README.md` names JSON-LD `isAccessibleForFree` the primary
path for that row and gives the reason: the page states its own access level
there, so it survives a class rename. The code and the doc disagree. Both
fixtures pass either way, so no test catches it. Decide before Milestone 2.


### 2026-08-24 - Capture the paywalled article fixture and confirm the paywall check

**What changed:** Captured `spike/fixtures/article-paywalled.html` (32 KB) from
`kevinszabo.substack.com`, a publication the reader does not pay for. Recorded
the paywall structure in `spike/README.md` under a new "Paywall block" section,
updated the metadata and body rows with paywalled values, and added four risks.
Added `expandPrivateStrings()` to `spike/capture-fixture.js`; `redact()` now
reads its terms from that function and sorts them longest-first.
**Why:** The capture leaked the reader's first name. The paywall greets the
reader with `Hi <b>Phillip</b>`, and `PRIVATE_STRINGS` held only the full name
`Phillip Lagoc`, which a literal match does not find inside a lone first name.
The leak was redacted in the fixture by hand and the snippet was widened so the
next capture catches it. The explicit term list was kept over deriving terms by
splitting on whitespace and punctuation: splitting the email address would add
`com` as a term and destroy every URL in the fixture.
**Files:** `spike/fixtures/article-paywalled.html`, `spike/README.md`,
`spike/capture-fixture.js`, `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-16-milestone-0.md`
**Verification:** `grep -ic "phillip|lagoc" spike/fixtures/article-paywalled.html`
returns 0. `node --check spike/capture-fixture.js` passes. `redact()` run over
the leak cases turns `Hi Phillip` into `Hi READER` and leaves
`The Writing Chronicles by Kevin Szabo` alone. `cd spike; npm test` passes.
**State after:** Both article fixtures are captured and clean. The paywall read
path is confirmed against both: JSON-LD `isAccessibleForFree` is `true` on the
free fixture and `false` on the paywalled one, and `.paywall` matches 0 and 1.
Word count cannot stand in for it: the paywalled preview holds 684 words, above
the 500-word floor the manifest uses for a free article. `manifest.json` is
still an empty list. Nothing is committed yet.
**Next up:** Task 3 Step 7, the signed-out check in a private window. Then Step 8
fills `manifest.json` from the values now recorded in `spike/README.md`, and
Step 9 commits.
**Open questions:** none


### 2026-08-24 - Capture the free article fixture and harden the anonymizer

**What changed:** Captured `spike/fixtures/article-free.html` (100 KB, 1599 body
words) from `escapethecubicle.substack.com`. Added `scrubIdentifiers()`,
`cleanUrl()`, and `cleanEmbedded()` to `spike/capture-fixture.js`, which run as a
new pass 3.5 before `redact()`. Filled the "body word count" and
"paywall / preview state" rows in `spike/README.md` and added five bullets to
"Risks found". Marked Task 3 Steps 1 to 5 complete in the plan.

**Why:** The first three captures leaked the reader's Substack `user_id`
(494698927). `redact()` matches literal strings, so it could not see the id in a
URL path, and could not see it at all inside a base64 share token nested in a
JSON blob in a `data-attrs` attribute. Three leak classes needed three fixes:
delete every query string, zero every 6-or-more digit run in a URL path, and
drop `data-attrs` outright. Dropping `data-attrs` beat parsing its JSON because
nothing downstream reads it: the word count, the button labels, and both button
target URLs all survive the delete, measured.

**Files:** `spike/capture-fixture.js`, `spike/fixtures/article-free.html`,
`spike/README.md`, `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-16-milestone-0.md`,
`docs/learning-notes.md`

**Verification:** `grep` over the fixture returns 0 for the user id, for any JWT,
for `token=`, for `data-attrs`, for the reader's name and email, and for any
query string or 6-digit run in a URL attribute. A linkedom parse confirms
`og:title`, `meta[name=author]`, `link[rel=canonical]`, `og:url`, and both
JSON-LD blocks read correctly, and that `.body.markup` still counts 1599 words,
the same as the unscrubbed capture. `npm test` not run: `extract.js` does not
exist yet.

**State after:** The free fixture is clean and committable. The extractor and its
fixture tests do not exist. `spike/fixtures/article-paywalled.html` holds only a
URL, and `manifest.json` is still an empty list, so Task 3 is not finished.

**Next up:** Task 3 Step 6. Capture the paywalled article from
`kevinszabo.substack.com` with the same snippet, then Step 7 (signed-out state),
then Step 8 (fill the manifest).

**Open questions:** The fixture keeps seven commenters' display names and profile
slugs. Their numeric ids are zeroed. The plan's constraint names the reader's
data only, so this is in bounds, but nothing reads those names either. Decide
whether a later capture strips them.

### 2026-08-19 - Cut paid subscriptions from Milestone 0 scope (commit 477f86e)

**What changed:** Removed the `article-paid-subscribed.html` fixture from the
Milestone 0 plan. Renamed `article-paid-preview.html` to
`article-paywalled.html`. Task 3 now captures two article fixtures, not three,
and its steps renumber from 10 down to 9. Added a "Deferred to a later version"
section to the plan and a scope line to Global Constraints. Updated the
"Supported desktop pages" table in the plan skeleton and in `spike/README.md`.
**Why:** The developer subscribes to no paid publication, so nobody can capture
a paid subscriber's article page. Milestone 0 is done when every fixture exists,
and a step that needs a paid account would hold the milestone open. Keeping the
step and marking it "skip" was rejected: a checklist with a permanent skip stops
being a checklist. The paywalled fixture stays because opening a paid article
you did not buy costs nothing, and `isPreview` needs that DOM shape. Without it
a 90-word teaser produces a "1 min read" card.
**Files:** `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-16-milestone-0.md`,
`spike/README.md`, `docs/learning-notes.md`.
**Verification:** `cd spike; npm test` passes the one smoke test. No fixture
test exists yet, so nothing else runs.
**State after:** The plan asks for three fixtures: `article-free.html`,
`article-paywalled.html`, `saved-list.html`. Task 12's definition of done counts
three. No fixture is captured yet.
**Next up:** Task 3, Step 1: pick the free article and the paywalled article.
**Open questions:** None.

### 2026-08-19 - Commit the learning notes and relax their writing rules (commit af1ea06)

**What changed:** Committed `docs/learning-notes.md` with the notes from Tasks 1
to 3, and the `CLAUDE.md` edit that governs them. Learning notes no longer
follow ASD-STE100. They follow the `stop-slop` skill and "explain like I'm 5".
**Why:** ASD-STE100 keeps every sentence short and uniform. That suits a task
plan, where a reader follows one instruction at a time. It fights a teaching
note, where a comparison or an analogy carries the point. The two rule sets
collided, and `CLAUDE.md` used to say ASD-STE100 wins. That line is gone.
**Files:** `CLAUDE.md`, `docs/learning-notes.md`.
**Verification:** Not applicable. No code changed.
**State after:** `AGENTS.md` still carries the old rules, so the two instruction
files disagree. Nothing else drifted.
**Next up:** Decide whether `AGENTS.md` copies the new rules or keeps ASD-STE100
for Codex.
**Open questions:** None.

